2. Access Modifiers
Modifier	Usage	Reason
private	Class fields	Protects data from direct access.
public	Getters and setters	Allows controlled access.
protected	Parent class methods	Allows access in subclasses.
default	Package-level methods	Restricts access within the same package.
3. Encapsulation Using Getters and Setters
Encapsulation means wrapping data and methods into a single class and protecting data using private fields.
Java Program:
class ParkingSlot {
    private int slotNo;
    private int duration;
    public int getSlotNo() {
        return slotNo;
    }

    public void setSlotNo(int slotNo) {
        if (slotNo > 0) {
            this.slotNo = slotNo;
        } else {
            System.out.println("Invalid slot number");
        }
    }

    public int getDuration() {
        return duration;
    }

    public void setDuration(int duration) {
        if (duration >= 0) {
            this.duration = duration;
        } else {
            System.out.println("Invalid duration");
        }
    }
}
Advantages:
•	Protects data from unauthorized modification.
•	Validates values before updating.
•	Improves security and maintainability.
4. Wrapper Classes and Autoboxing
Wrapper classes convert primitive data types into objects.
Example:
Integer slotNo = 10;  // Autoboxing
int slot = slotNo;    // Unboxing
Risk: Comparing Integer objects using == may give incorrect results because it can compare object references instead of values.
Correct method:
Integer a = 1000;
Integer b = 1000;
System.out.println(a.equals(b)); // true
5. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
If fields are public, any class can directly modify them without validation.
Example:
public int duration = -10;
This can create invalid parking records.
Comparison	Private Fields	Public Fields
Data access	Through getters/setters	Direct access
Validation	Inside setter	Repeated in calling code
Security	Better protection	Less protection
Maintenance	Easy	Difficult
Validation time	O(1) per update	O(1) per check
For n updates, validation takes O(n) time in both approaches, but public fields may require repeating the same validation in multiple places.

